everything has a rhythm whether you're  fighting whether you're walking whether  you're typing on a computer you will  naturally fall into a rhythm it takes  far more conscious effort not to adopt a  rhythm than it does to adopt a rhythm  our ability to observe rhythms to create  beats is highly important then to our  psychological function and our  performance in athletics martial arts  and more so in parkour we need rhythm  because when we're running towards a  wall or a ledge we need to fall into the  cadence and adopt a specific rhythm in  order to make sure that our footing is  correct once you're at that right point  otherwise we jump too early or too late  or stumble over ourselves and faceplant  in martial arts or boxing you need to  adopt a rhythm and you need to adapt to  the opponent's rhythm and then find  breaks in that pattern in order to  sucker punch them and catch them  unawares it's an incredibly valuable  technique when we're typing when we're  running we fall into a natural cadence  and this rhythm helps us to perform our  very best and when predicting the  movements of things in the real world  around us we need to use rhythm in order  to predict where they'll be at a given  time where you're watching something fly  through the air it has a kind of rhythm  it's the same mechanisms that you're  using in your brain you ability to  predict movement over time and to  anticipate those patterns and in case  you think I'm just blowing smoke there  are studies demonstrating that rhythm  training can improve the performance of  tennis players versus those who've  engaged in no such timing again because  it helps them presumably to predict the  movement of the ball and to fall into a  rhythm with their opponent that they can  then exploit later on a particularly  obvious example of beat and rhythm being  useful in terms of performance is in  computer games you've ever played the  game where there's a bouncing object you  have to run through it at the right time  or a laser that goes on and off this  demonstrates how being able to predict a  pattern go help you to avoid a hazard  and lots of computer games are built  around this concept one of my original  examples of why we need rhythm was that  in real life you might find yourself  faced with a stalactite that's dropping  in a cave a certain rhythm you have to  dive under it then I realized that  literally will never happen to anyone so  the ability to predict and sense rhythm  really does have the multiple benefit  whether you're looking at sports  six martial arts parkour gymnastics or  even something like long-distance  running or typing long essays we're  falling to a rhythm helps you to get  into a kind of groove and more easily  enter a flow state and is there  something we don't all have to the same  degree we've already seen that you can  train to improve your rhythm and some  people are actually unable to sense  rhythm at all I haven't mate who is weak  to death and this means he can't hear  and adapt to a beat and he looks like a  complete tosser on the dance floor this  might actually be by the way why we find  people who can dance well attractive  because an ability to sense of rhythm is  a trait that has a survival value okay  so where in the brain is rhythm located  but on the one hand you've got your  auditory cortex which of course handles  auditory information so it makes sense  that this would be useful for sensing a  rhythm and indeed damage to this area  will impair your ability to sense peace  however beats and rhythms aren't only  auditory they can also be visual as  we've seen and in fact my friends whose  be deaf interestingly says that he's  much better at keeping time when he sees  something visual so he can dance by  watching other people dance and  mimicking them like a gorilla  so like I've said it also seems that an  ability to sense rhythms and beats is  connected to our ability to sense the  passage of time in general and this  might be further insight into why it's  just so valuable and the areas of the  brain that are responsible it seems for  sensing the passage of time are the  cerebellum the cerebral cortex and the  basal ganglia and again damage to these  parts of the brain can impair our  ability not only to sense rhythms but to  sense the passage of time in general fun  fact I used to be able to perfectly cook  an egg without using a timer I no longer  have that ability my eggs come out bad  what's really interesting to me is the  fact that our ability to sense rhythm  and the passage of time seems to be  related closely to ability to visualize  in general and this is in keeping with  some of the things I've talked about  recently on this channel so when we  predict how long the movements going to  take or when we try to engage in some  kind of physical rhythm like a dance we  actually use the motor cortex or the  premotor cortex to estimate how long  that's going to take and how long the  gaps in between those movements are but  when we estimate how long something  visuals gonna take when we try and  predict a flashing light for instance  that actually uses the  cortex so it seems that we're literally  simulating the action in our brain and  how accurate that simulation is  determines just how accurate our  prediction of that rhythm or that beats  gonna be so it might be that improving  your beat could have crossover effects  and improve your general visualization  your general embodied cognition and your  whole ability to think it's more  complicated than that we don't really  understand how it all works because in  one study rats had their entire cortices  removed and they still had some  lingering remnants of time perceptions  remaining so since this is a really  innate ability that might be very  unconscious to a certain extent which is  why we fall into a rhythm when we walk  or when we're breathing and that means  that some of these other processes are  kind of layered on top of 